1.4.5 Release
-------------

In order to get special characters working properly, several changes were 
necessary to have Unicode implemented. You need to do a few steps to update 
an existing installation (new installations should work with Unicode out of 
the box):
* Do a "SET CHARACTER SET utf8" in the mysql client. This should change various 
settings in mysql. You can check if this is necessary by doing a "show variables like "character_set%";" and 
"show variables like "collation%";". If this says utf8 everywhere, no change is needed. If you are using 
mysl 5.5.3 or later, use utf8mb4 instead of utf8 everywhere (utf8 in mysql isn't utf8m, 
it only handles 3 byte characters, utf8mb4 is proper utf8, but only available from 5.5.3).
* The my.cnf file should have
[mysqld] 
character-set-server = utf8
collation-server = utf8_unicode_ci
in it
* Existing tables must be changed to utf8 with "ALTER TABLE tbl_name CONVERT TO CHARACTER SET utf8 COLLATE utf8_general_ci;"
In theory, it would be enough to convert tables which happen to have "freetext" in them 
(like CHEMICAL_NAME), but I would recommend to do it for all tables.
* Unfortunately, the text columns must also be changed indiviually. http://codex.wordpress.org/Converting_Database_Character_Sets 
seems to be a good guide on how to do this.
* Now unfortunately in the past some character (namely alpha and beta) were inserted 
in tables directly as &#945; and &#946; These must be replaced by the unicode characters alpha and beta.
* Everything should work now. It is also important to add useUnicode=true&characterEncoding=UTF-8 to the connection 
string for jdbc, but this is done if you use the config files from the nmrshiftdb2 source.

Issues with isotope numbers:
For unknown reasons, in the past isotope numbers where not correctly saved (many Cs got 6 etc.). This 
didn't really hurt, since the isotopes where not displayed or exported. I fixed this to a) proper save 
isotope numbers and b) show/export them. This means the old numbers need to be fixed. I suggest to use 
the most common isotope, C=12 and so on. Since isotopes where in effect not handled in the past, there 
should not be a real loss.

